\b\fs28 C. Pose-Correction Efficacy Study (Proposed)\b0\fs22\par
To measure whether the hysteresis-filtered coach actually improves alignment versus a no-feedback control, we propose a within-subject design:\par\par
\bullet  Recruit $n = 60$ novice yoga learners.\par
\bullet  Randomise pose-and-condition assignment so each learner experiences each of three conditions on three different poses: *(C1)* no feedback, *(C2)* unfiltered immediate feedback, *(C3)* hysteresis-filtered feedback.\par
\bullet  Outcome: mean alignment score over a 60-second hold; secondary outcome: self-reported workload (NASA-TLX).\par
\bullet  Hypothesis: $s_\{C3\} > s_\{C2\} > s_\{C1\}$ for mean score, and $\\text\{TLX\}_\{C3\} < \\text\{TLX\}_\{C2\}$ for workload \u2014 i.e., the filter helps both alignment and perceived ease.\par
\par
The study protocol is registered and will be reported in a follow-up paper.\par\par
\b\fs32 VII. Discussion\b0\fs22\par
\b\fs28 A. Limitations\b0\fs22\par
**Single-camera limitation.** A monocular camera cannot recover absolute depth. Poses requiring lateral hip rotation visible only in profile (e.g., Trikonasana from the front) suffer from foreshortening and produce unreliable angle estimates. We work around this by encoding *ratios* of distances rather than absolute distances where possible, but the underlying ambiguity remains.\par\par
**Inversions.** MoveNet's training distribution is biased toward upright posture. Headstand (Sirsasana) and shoulderstand (Sarvangasana) are excluded from the certificate course for this reason. A future version may use a fine-tuned MoveNet head trained on inverted poses.\par\par
**WebSpeech browser fragmentation.** Voice recognition is uneven across browsers: Chrome routes through Google's cloud STT (good accuracy, requires internet); Safari uses on-device STT (lower accuracy on technical Sanskrit terms but offline). We do not currently expose this trade-off to the user.\par\par
\b\fs28 B. Ethical Considerations\b0\fs22\par
**Privacy-by-default.** No video frame, no keypoint, no inference result ever leaves the user's device. The server only sees session metadata (pose name, peak score, duration) and never raw imagery. This makes the system suitable for institutional deployment without requiring DPDP-Act compliance plumbing for raw biometric data.\par\par
**Accessibility and bias.** Pose models are trained on COCO, which under-represents larger body sizes, dark skin tones in low light, and non-binary body shapes. We mitigate by (a) using rule-based scoring that is more forgiving than a learned classifier, (b) running model inference at user-controlled frame quality, (c) providing a manual "good enough" override that lets a learner advance if the model mis-scores them. Long-term bias measurement is part of our ethics-review pipeline but is not yet quantified.\par\par
**Medical disclaimer.** AsanMinds is wellness software, not medical advice. The interface displays contraindications for each pose (e.g., Setu Bandhasana \u2014 avoid in pregnancy or recent neck injury) and asks new users to acknowledge a fitness disclaimer.\par\par
\b\fs32 VIII. Conclusion\b0\fs22\par
We have presented AsanMinds, a browser-native yoga coaching system that (a) runs MoveNet SinglePose Thunder fully on-device for privacy and connectivity-tolerance, (b) converts per-frame rule evaluations into deliberate spoken corrections via a hysteresis filter, (c) supports a hands-free voice command grammar with priority-based disambiguation, and (d) scales to multi-tenant institutional use with domain-scoped data isolation and a six-semester certificate programme.\par\par
Open-source code, deployment URL, and the pose rule sets for all twenty curated asanas are available at the project repository. Future work includes a within-subject efficacy study (Section VI.C), extension to inversions via fine-tuned MoveNet, and an offline WebSpeech replacement using on-device Whisper.\par\par
